//! examples/cayley_dickson_sedentree_realtree16_training.sio
//!
//! First TRAINED (not just algebra-verified) re-run of the paper's §3.3/§9.4
//! SedenTree claim in docs/papers/main/cayley_dickson_hierarchy_paper_2026-08-13.md:
//! that a 16-dimensional sedenion-product tree (SedenTree) solves a
//! nested-vs-crossed structural discrimination task that a dimensionally-
//! matched elementwise-product control (RealTree-16) cannot, because the
//! sedenion product is non-alternative and can therefore represent
//! parenthesization-order (nesting vs crossing) that a commutative product
//! cannot. This is the SedenTree counterpart to
//! examples/cayley_dickson_octtree_realtree_training.sio's OctTree-vs-
//! RealTree-8 result on Dyck-1; that file's §9.4 Python re-run (not yet in
//! Sounio before this file) found SedenTree-16=0.992 vs RealTree-16=0.500
//! (chance) on a real, non-degenerate pseudoknot generator at length 32.
//!
//! WHY A NEW MINIMAL TASK, NOT scripts/research/pseudoknot_experiment.py's
//! full length-32 generator or examples/cayley_dickson_dyck_pseudoknot_
//! generators.sio's gen_pseudoknot (also length-32): a length-32 sequence
//! needs a depth-5 tree (2^5=32 leaves), and each internal SedenTree node
//! costs roughly 4x an OctTree node's tape footprint (measured below,
//! ~518 vs ~120 nodes per multiply, because the sedenion product is
//! implemented as 4 nested octonion products via the Cayley-Dickson
//! half-split, mirroring stdlib/algebra/sedenion.sio's sed_vv). A depth-5,
//! 16-dim tree would need on the order of 31 internal sed16_mul calls per
//! forward pass (~16,000 tape nodes) -- far past any tape budget this
//! investigation has found safe (examples/cayley_dickson_octtree_
//! realtree_training.sio's 768-node, 8-dim, depth-2 tree already sits near
//! a demonstrated resource ceiling at just 4 seeds). Per this
//! investigation's established discipline (try, measure, scale down,
//! document -- never silently fabricate a smaller-but-unlabeled result),
//! this file uses the smallest sequence length where the nested-vs-crossed
//! distinction exists at all: length 4, depth-2 (the SedenTree analogue of
//! the OctTree file's Dyck-4 minimal case), with two bracket types packed
//! as tightly as possible ("([])" = nested vs "([)]" = crossed) rather
//! than the paper's length-32 sweep. This is explicitly a smaller-scope
//! proof-of-concept, not a replacement for the retracted/§9.4 numbers.
//!
//! CORRECTNESS OF sed16_mul ON THE TAPE: rather than hand-deriving 16
//! chains of up to 15 signed terms each (the failure-prone pattern that
//! produced the audited-broken sedenion table flagged throughout this
//! investigation's §9.2), sed16_mul is built by composing the ALREADY
//! gradient-verified oct8_mul (from the OctTree file, reproduced here)
//! exactly the way stdlib/algebra/sedenion.sio's sed_vv composes its own
//! oct_p/oct_q: for a=(a_lo,a_hi), b=(b_lo,b_hi),
//!   lower = oct_mul(a_lo, b_lo) - oct_mul(conj(b_hi), a_hi)
//!   upper = oct_mul(b_hi, a_lo) + oct_mul(a_hi, conj(b_lo))
//! This is verified two ways before any training result is trusted (see
//! verify_sed16() in main()): (1) e_i * e_i = -e_0 for i=1..15, and (2)
//! e_i * e_j = -(e_j * e_i) for all 1<=i<j<=15 (anticommutativity on
//! imaginary units) -- the SAME two identities this investigation's
//! count_anticommutativity_violations() uses to certify a sedenion table,
//! evaluated here via the tape's own forward pass (do_update=false, node
//! values read directly), not a separate implementation.
//!
//! RESULT (2026-08-15, seed=20260815, N_train=40, N_test=20, 15 epochs,
//! plain SGD, LR=0.05): a NULL result on the real task, not a positive
//! one -- both SedenTree and RealTree-16 are stuck at exactly 0.500
//! test/train accuracy (chance) at every logged checkpoint (epoch 1, 5,
//! 10, 15), with SedenTree's mean training loss essentially flat (0.622
//! -> 0.690 -> 0.690 -> 0.687, i.e. converging to a constant-probability
//! output, not learning a decision boundary) -- reproduced byte-for-byte
//! across three separate runs, including one extended to 25 epochs that
//! showed no change in the pattern by epoch 10.
//!
//! Before trusting that as "the task is hard" rather than "the loop is
//! broken," main() now runs a SANITY CHECK first: the identical
//! architecture/optimizer trained on a trivially separable task (label =
//! token[0], 3 random noise tokens) instead of gen_pk4. Result: train_acc
//! 0.500 -> 0.825 -> 1.000 -> 1.000 and test_acc 0.600 -> 0.800 -> 0.950
//! -> 1.000 over the same 15 epochs -- the loop, gradient computation,
//! and optimizer all genuinely work. This makes the real task's null
//! result a real finding, not a masked bug: this particular minimal
//! proof-of-concept (no learnable gates, plain SGD, 40 training examples,
//! one combine level's worth of capacity, genuine 2-level sequence-order
//! reasoning required) is too weak a setup for either model to solve this
//! specific task, at least at this scale/seed -- unlike the OctTree/
//! Dyck-4 file's result (a real, seed-dependent-but-nonzero OctTree>
//! RealTree gap on an architecturally similar but different task), which
//! DID learn at a comparable scale.
//!
//! The genuinely load-bearing output of building this file was not a
//! training result either way: it is sed16_mul itself (verified correct,
//! 120/120 identities, via composing the already-gradient-verified
//! oct8_mul exactly as stdlib/algebra/sedenion.sio's sed_vv does) and
//! FOUR new Sounio/Madaros compiler bugs found and fixed while getting
//! there (see the trailing comment block): (1) same-shaped local array
//! `let`-bindings alias each other's storage within one function; (2) a
//! too-small TAPE_CAP silently wraps writes instead of erroring (tpush()
//! now hard-aborts on overflow); (3) a `[[T;N];M]` nested-array global
//! corrupted an ADJACENT global's memory; (4) a module-level `var
//! SEED_LIST: [i64;N]` read with a runtime loop index in the multi-seed
//! summary always returned element 0 (both seeds printed as "20260815"
//! instead of 20260815 then 7) -- the same defect class already found in
//! examples/cayley_dickson_lemon_g2_ffi.sio, fixed the same way (made
//! local to main(), no threading needed since it's only read there).
//! Scaling the real task up (more training data, the paper's learnable
//! gates) to find out whether either model CAN learn a bigger version of
//! it is not yet done -- flagged here, not attempted, given the per-step
//! compute cost sed16_mul's ~510-node tape footprint already imposes (a
//! single 15-epoch/40-example run takes several minutes wall-clock).
//!
//! Run: scripts/dev/souc-build-lock.sh make build   # -> ./gen3.elf, fixed-point-verified
//!      SOUNIO_STDLIB_PATH=$(pwd)/stdlib ./gen3.elf \
//!        examples/cayley_dickson_sedentree_realtree16_training.sio /tmp/sed.elf && /tmp/sed.elf
//!      (bin/souc-lean-single-x86_64 is a stale unrelated tool, not a
//!      lean_single build -- see docs/audit/EXTERN_C_FFI_SILENT_NOOP_DISPATCH_2026-08-13.md)

// =============================================================================
// PRINT HELPERS
// =============================================================================

fn print_i64(x: i64) with IO, Mut, Div, Panic {
    if x < 0 { print("-"); print_i64(0 - x) }
    else if x < 10 { print_digit(x) }
    else { print_i64(x / 10); print_digit(x - (x / 10) * 10) }
}
fn print_digit(d: i64) with IO {
    if d == 0 { print("0") } else if d == 1 { print("1") }
    else if d == 2 { print("2") } else if d == 3 { print("3") }
    else if d == 4 { print("4") } else if d == 5 { print("5") }
    else if d == 6 { print("6") } else if d == 7 { print("7") }
    else if d == 8 { print("8") } else { print("9") }
}
fn print_f64(x: f64, decimals: i64) with IO, Mut, Div, Panic {
    if x < 0.0 { print("-"); print_f64(0.0 - x, decimals) }
    else {
        let int_part = x as i64
        print_i64(int_part)
        print(".")
        var frac = x - (int_part as f64)
        var d: i64 = 0
        while d < decimals {
            frac = frac * 10.0
            let digit = frac as i64
            print_digit(digit)
            frac = frac - (digit as f64)
            d = d + 1
        }
    }
}

// =============================================================================
// PRNG (SplitMix64, same construction proven throughout this investigation)
// =============================================================================

var RNG_STATE: [i64; 1] = [0; 1]
fn rng_seed(seed: i64) with Mut { RNG_STATE[0] = seed }
fn rng_next_u64() -> i64 with Mut {
    RNG_STATE[0] = RNG_STATE[0] + 0x9e3779b97f4a7c15 as i64
    var z = RNG_STATE[0]
    z = z ^ (z >> 30)
    z = z * 0xbf58476d as i64
    z = z ^ (z >> 27)
    z = z * 0x94d049bb as i64
    z = z ^ (z >> 31)
    z
}
fn rng_nonneg(bound: i64) -> i64 with Mut, Div {
    let u = rng_next_u64() & 0x7fffffff
    u - (u / bound) * bound
}
fn rng_bit() -> bool with Mut, Div { rng_nonneg(2) == 1 }
fn rng_next_signed() -> f64 with Mut, Div {
    let u = rng_next_u64() & 0x7fffffff
    let v = (u as f64) / 1073741823.5
    v - 1.0
}

// =============================================================================
// LOCAL REVERSE-MODE AUTOGRAD TAPE
// Same op codes / backward rules as examples/cayley_dickson_octtree_
// realtree_training.sio, capacity raised 768 -> 4096: a depth-2 SedenTree
// forward pass measures ~1794 nodes (4 leaves x 16 dims = 64, 3 sed16_mul
// calls x ~510 nodes = 1530, 3 vec16_add + 3 vec16_tanh x 16 = 96,
// readout ~98, cross-entropy ~6); verify_sed16()'s antisymmetry check
// measures ~1084 (two 16-node basis vectors + one sed16_mul + two more
// basis rebuilds + a second sed16_mul). 4096 leaves >2x margin over the
// larger of the two -- tpush() below hard-aborts if this is ever
// exceeded rather than silently overwriting index 0, after this file's
// own investigation found that a too-small TAPE_CAP (1024, from an
// earlier debugging step) silently wrapped writes and produced a
// plausible-looking but wrong correctness-check result.
// =============================================================================

fn OPV() -> i32 { 0 }
fn OPADD() -> i32 { 1 }
fn OPMUL() -> i32 { 2 }
fn OPSUB() -> i32 { 3 }
fn OPTANH() -> i32 { 4 }
fn OPEXP() -> i32 { 5 }
fn OPLOG() -> i32 { 6 }
fn OPNEG() -> i32 { 7 }
fn OPDIV() -> i32 { 8 }

const TAPE_CAP: i64 = 4096

struct Tape {
    values: [f64; 4096],
    grads: [f64; 4096],
    ops: [i32; 4096],
    arg1: [i32; 4096],
    arg2: [i32; 4096],
    len: i32,
}

fn tape_new() -> Tape {
    Tape {
        values: [0.0; 4096], grads: [0.0; 4096], ops: [0; 4096],
        arg1: [0 - 1; 4096], arg2: [0 - 1; 4096], len: 0,
    }
}

fn tpush(t: Tape, op: i32, val: f64, a: i32, b: i32) -> (i32, Tape) with Mut, Panic {
    var t2 = t
    let idx = t2.len
    if (idx as i64) >= TAPE_CAP { panic("tape overflow: TAPE_CAP exceeded -- raise the constant, do not trust any output past this point") }
    t2.ops[idx as usize] = op
    t2.values[idx as usize] = val
    t2.grads[idx as usize] = 0.0
    t2.arg1[idx as usize] = a
    t2.arg2[idx as usize] = b
    t2.len = t2.len + 1
    (idx, t2)
}

fn tvar(t: Tape, v: f64) -> (i32, Tape) with Mut, Panic {
    tpush(t, OPV(), v, 0 - 1, 0 - 1)
}
fn tadd(t: Tape, a: i32, b: i32) -> (i32, Tape) with Mut, Div, Panic {
    tpush(t, OPADD(), t.values[a as usize] + t.values[b as usize], a, b)
}
fn tsub(t: Tape, a: i32, b: i32) -> (i32, Tape) with Mut, Div, Panic {
    tpush(t, OPSUB(), t.values[a as usize] - t.values[b as usize], a, b)
}
fn tmul(t: Tape, a: i32, b: i32) -> (i32, Tape) with Mut, Div, Panic {
    tpush(t, OPMUL(), t.values[a as usize] * t.values[b as usize], a, b)
}
fn tdiv(t: Tape, a: i32, b: i32) -> (i32, Tape) with Mut, Div, Panic {
    tpush(t, OPDIV(), t.values[a as usize] / t.values[b as usize], a, b)
}
fn ttanh_f64(x: f64) -> f64 with Mut, Div, Panic {
    if x > 20.0 { return 1.0 }
    if x < 0.0 - 20.0 { return 0.0 - 1.0 }
    let e2x = texp_f64(2.0 * x)
    (e2x - 1.0) / (e2x + 1.0)
}
fn texp_f64(x: f64) -> f64 with Mut, Div, Panic {
    if x > 20.0 { return texp_f64(x / 2.0) * texp_f64(x / 2.0) }
    if x < 0.0 - 20.0 { return 1.0 / texp_f64(0.0 - x) }
    var sum = 1.0; var term = 1.0; var i: i64 = 1
    while i <= 20 { term = term * x / (i as f64); sum = sum + term; i = i + 1 }
    sum
}
fn tln_f64(x: f64) -> f64 with Mut, Div, Panic {
    if x <= 0.0 { return 0.0 - 1000000.0 }
    if x > 1.5 { return 2.0 * tln_f64(sqrt_f64_local(x)) }
    if x < 0.7 { return 0.0 - tln_f64(1.0 / x) }
    let u = x - 1.0
    var sum = 0.0; var term = u; var i: i64 = 1
    while i <= 30 { sum = sum + term / (i as f64); term = 0.0 - term * u; i = i + 1 }
    sum
}
fn sqrt_f64_local(x: f64) -> f64 with Mut, Div, Panic {
    if x <= 0.0 { return 0.0 }
    var g = x; var i: i64 = 0
    while i < 25 { g = (g + x / g) / 2.0; i = i + 1 }
    g
}
fn ttanh(t: Tape, a: i32) -> (i32, Tape) with Mut, Div, Panic {
    tpush(t, OPTANH(), ttanh_f64(t.values[a as usize]), a, 0 - 1)
}
fn texp(t: Tape, a: i32) -> (i32, Tape) with Mut, Div, Panic {
    tpush(t, OPEXP(), texp_f64(t.values[a as usize]), a, 0 - 1)
}
fn tlog(t: Tape, a: i32) -> (i32, Tape) with Mut, Div, Panic {
    tpush(t, OPLOG(), tln_f64(t.values[a as usize]), a, 0 - 1)
}
fn tneg(t: Tape, a: i32) -> (i32, Tape) with Mut, Div, Panic {
    tpush(t, OPNEG(), 0.0 - t.values[a as usize], a, 0 - 1)
}

fn tape_backward(t: Tape, output: i32) -> Tape with Mut, Div, Panic {
    var t2 = t
    t2.grads[output as usize] = 1.0
    var i = output
    while i >= 0 {
        let op = t2.ops[i as usize]
        let g = t2.grads[i as usize]
        let a = t2.arg1[i as usize]
        let b = t2.arg2[i as usize]
        let va = if a >= 0 { t2.values[a as usize] } else { 0.0 }
        let vb = if b >= 0 { t2.values[b as usize] } else { 0.0 }
        let vout = t2.values[i as usize]
        if op == OPADD() {
            if a >= 0 { t2.grads[a as usize] = t2.grads[a as usize] + g }
            if b >= 0 { t2.grads[b as usize] = t2.grads[b as usize] + g }
        }
        if op == OPSUB() {
            if a >= 0 { t2.grads[a as usize] = t2.grads[a as usize] + g }
            if b >= 0 { t2.grads[b as usize] = t2.grads[b as usize] - g }
        }
        if op == OPMUL() {
            if a >= 0 { t2.grads[a as usize] = t2.grads[a as usize] + g * vb }
            if b >= 0 { t2.grads[b as usize] = t2.grads[b as usize] + g * va }
        }
        if op == OPDIV() {
            if a >= 0 { t2.grads[a as usize] = t2.grads[a as usize] + g / vb }
            if b >= 0 { t2.grads[b as usize] = t2.grads[b as usize] - g * va / (vb * vb) }
        }
        if op == OPTANH() {
            if a >= 0 { t2.grads[a as usize] = t2.grads[a as usize] + g * (1.0 - vout * vout) }
        }
        if op == OPEXP() {
            if a >= 0 { t2.grads[a as usize] = t2.grads[a as usize] + g * vout }
        }
        if op == OPLOG() {
            if a >= 0 { t2.grads[a as usize] = t2.grads[a as usize] + g / va }
        }
        if op == OPNEG() {
            if a >= 0 { t2.grads[a as usize] = t2.grads[a as usize] - g }
        }
        i = i - 1
    }
    t2
}

// =============================================================================
// OCTONION MULTIPLY ON THE TAPE (identical to the OctTree file's oct8_mul,
// reproduced here since this file is self-contained -- no cross-file
// imports, matching this investigation's established convention)
// =============================================================================

fn chain8(t: Tape, t0: i32, t1: i32, s1: bool, t2: i32, s2: bool, t3: i32, s3: bool,
          t4: i32, s4: bool, t5: i32, s5: bool, t6: i32, s6: bool, t7: i32, s7: bool
) -> (i32, Tape) with Mut, Div, Panic {
    var tt = t
    var acc = t0
    if s1 { let (i, tn) = tadd(tt, acc, t1); tt = tn; acc = i } else { let (i, tn) = tsub(tt, acc, t1); tt = tn; acc = i }
    if s2 { let (i, tn) = tadd(tt, acc, t2); tt = tn; acc = i } else { let (i, tn) = tsub(tt, acc, t2); tt = tn; acc = i }
    if s3 { let (i, tn) = tadd(tt, acc, t3); tt = tn; acc = i } else { let (i, tn) = tsub(tt, acc, t3); tt = tn; acc = i }
    if s4 { let (i, tn) = tadd(tt, acc, t4); tt = tn; acc = i } else { let (i, tn) = tsub(tt, acc, t4); tt = tn; acc = i }
    if s5 { let (i, tn) = tadd(tt, acc, t5); tt = tn; acc = i } else { let (i, tn) = tsub(tt, acc, t5); tt = tn; acc = i }
    if s6 { let (i, tn) = tadd(tt, acc, t6); tt = tn; acc = i } else { let (i, tn) = tsub(tt, acc, t6); tt = tn; acc = i }
    if s7 { let (i, tn) = tadd(tt, acc, t7); tt = tn; acc = i } else { let (i, tn) = tsub(tt, acc, t7); tt = tn; acc = i }
    (acc, tt)
}

fn oct8_mul(t: Tape, a: [i32; 8], b: [i32; 8]) -> ([i32; 8], Tape) with Mut, Div, Panic {
    var tt = t
    var out: [i32; 8] = [0; 8]
    var p: [i32; 64] = [0; 64]
    var i: i64 = 0
    while i < 8 {
        var j: i64 = 0
        while j < 8 {
            let (idx, tn) = tmul(tt, a[i as usize], b[j as usize])
            tt = tn
            p[(i * 8 + j) as usize] = idx
            j = j + 1
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    let (r0, t0) = chain8(tt, p[0], p[9],false, p[18],false, p[27],false, p[36],false, p[45],false, p[54],false, p[63],false); tt = t0
    out[0] = r0
    let (r1, t1) = chain8(tt, p[1], p[8],true, p[19],true, p[26],false, p[37],true, p[44],false, p[55],false, p[62],true); tt = t1
    out[1] = r1
    let (r2, t2) = chain8(tt, p[2], p[16],true, p[11],false, p[25],true, p[38],true, p[52],false, p[47],true, p[61],false); tt = t2
    out[2] = r2
    let (r3, t3) = chain8(tt, p[3], p[24],true, p[10],true, p[17],false, p[39],true, p[60],false, p[46],false, p[53],true); tt = t3
    out[3] = r3
    let (r4, t4) = chain8(tt, p[4], p[32],true, p[13],false, p[41],true, p[22],false, p[50],true, p[31],false, p[59],true); tt = t4
    out[4] = r4
    let (r5, t5) = chain8(tt, p[5], p[40],true, p[12],true, p[33],false, p[23],false, p[58],true, p[30],true, p[51],false); tt = t5
    out[5] = r5
    let (r6, t6) = chain8(tt, p[6], p[48],true, p[15],true, p[57],false, p[20],true, p[34],false, p[29],false, p[43],true); tt = t6
    out[6] = r6
    let (r7, t7) = chain8(tt, p[7], p[56],true, p[14],false, p[49],true, p[21],true, p[42],false, p[28],true, p[35],false); tt = t7
    out[7] = r7
    (out, tt)
}

fn real8_mul(t: Tape, a: [i32; 8], b: [i32; 8]) -> ([i32; 8], Tape) with Mut, Div, Panic {
    var tt = t
    var out: [i32; 8] = [0; 8]
    var i: i64 = 0
    while i < 8 {
        let (idx, tn) = tmul(tt, a[i as usize], b[i as usize])
        tt = tn
        out[i as usize] = idx
        i = i + 1
    }
    (out, tt)
}

fn vec8_add(t: Tape, a: [i32; 8], b: [i32; 8]) -> ([i32; 8], Tape) with Mut, Div, Panic {
    var tt = t
    var out: [i32; 8] = [0; 8]
    var i: i64 = 0
    while i < 8 { let (idx, tn) = tadd(tt, a[i as usize], b[i as usize]); tt = tn; out[i as usize] = idx; i = i + 1 }
    (out, tt)
}
fn vec8_sub(t: Tape, a: [i32; 8], b: [i32; 8]) -> ([i32; 8], Tape) with Mut, Div, Panic {
    var tt = t
    var out: [i32; 8] = [0; 8]
    var i: i64 = 0
    while i < 8 { let (idx, tn) = tsub(tt, a[i as usize], b[i as usize]); tt = tn; out[i as usize] = idx; i = i + 1 }
    (out, tt)
}
fn conj8(t: Tape, x: [i32; 8]) -> ([i32; 8], Tape) with Mut, Div, Panic {
    var tt = t
    var out: [i32; 8] = [0; 8]
    out[0] = x[0]
    var i: i64 = 1
    while i < 8 { let (idx, tn) = tneg(tt, x[i as usize]); tt = tn; out[i as usize] = idx; i = i + 1 }
    (out, tt)
}

// =============================================================================
// SEDENION MULTIPLY ON THE TAPE, 16-dim, via the Cayley-Dickson half-split
// -- mirrors stdlib/algebra/sedenion.sio's sed_vv exactly (see header):
//   a=(a_lo,a_hi), b=(b_lo,b_hi)
//   lower = oct_mul(a_lo,b_lo) - oct_mul(conj(b_hi), a_hi)
//   upper = oct_mul(b_hi,a_lo) + oct_mul(a_hi, conj(b_lo))
// =============================================================================

// NOTE: every [i32;8]/[i32;16] intermediate below is copied into a
// freshly-`var`-declared, elementwise-loop-filled local IMMEDIATELY after
// the call that produces it, rather than kept as the `let`-bound tuple
// result. This works around a genuine compiler bug found while building
// this file: two `let`-bound array-typed locals of the SAME shape,
// declared in the same function, alias the same storage once a second one
// is declared -- reading the FIRST one again after the SECOND is computed
// silently returns the SECOND's values (confirmed via direct index prints:
// immediately after computing it, `p[0]` was tape-index 86 with value
// -1.0; after computing a same-shaped `q` later in the same function and
// reading `p[0]` again with no intervening reassignment, it read back as
// tape-index 213 -- literally q[0]'s own index -- value 0.0). A `var`
// declared directly and filled via an explicit index loop does not
// exhibit this; a `let` bound to any function's `[i32;8]`-returning call
// does, regardless of which function. Not yet forensic-dispatched --
// flagged here for that process, worked around rather than patched in
// self-hosted/, per this investigation's standing protocol.
fn sed16_mul(t: Tape, a: [i32; 16], b: [i32; 16]) -> ([i32; 16], Tape) with Mut, Div, Panic {
    var tt = t
    var a_lo: [i32; 8] = [0; 8]
    var a_hi: [i32; 8] = [0; 8]
    var b_lo: [i32; 8] = [0; 8]
    var b_hi: [i32; 8] = [0; 8]
    var i: i64 = 0
    while i < 8 {
        a_lo[i as usize] = a[i as usize]
        a_hi[i as usize] = a[(8 + i) as usize]
        b_lo[i as usize] = b[i as usize]
        b_hi[i as usize] = b[(8 + i) as usize]
        i = i + 1
    }

    let (p_raw, t1) = oct8_mul(tt, a_lo, b_lo); tt = t1
    var p: [i32; 8] = [0; 8]
    i = 0; while i < 8 { p[i as usize] = p_raw[i as usize]; i = i + 1 }

    let (bhi_c_raw, t2) = conj8(tt, b_hi); tt = t2
    var bhi_c: [i32; 8] = [0; 8]
    i = 0; while i < 8 { bhi_c[i as usize] = bhi_c_raw[i as usize]; i = i + 1 }

    let (q_raw, t3) = oct8_mul(tt, bhi_c, a_hi); tt = t3
    var q: [i32; 8] = [0; 8]
    i = 0; while i < 8 { q[i as usize] = q_raw[i as usize]; i = i + 1 }

    let (lo_raw, t4) = vec8_sub(tt, p, q); tt = t4
    var lo: [i32; 8] = [0; 8]
    i = 0; while i < 8 { lo[i as usize] = lo_raw[i as usize]; i = i + 1 }

    let (p2_raw, t5) = oct8_mul(tt, b_hi, a_lo); tt = t5
    var p2: [i32; 8] = [0; 8]
    i = 0; while i < 8 { p2[i as usize] = p2_raw[i as usize]; i = i + 1 }

    let (blo_c_raw, t6) = conj8(tt, b_lo); tt = t6
    var blo_c: [i32; 8] = [0; 8]
    i = 0; while i < 8 { blo_c[i as usize] = blo_c_raw[i as usize]; i = i + 1 }

    let (q2_raw, t7) = oct8_mul(tt, a_hi, blo_c); tt = t7
    var q2: [i32; 8] = [0; 8]
    i = 0; while i < 8 { q2[i as usize] = q2_raw[i as usize]; i = i + 1 }

    let (hi_raw, t8) = vec8_add(tt, p2, q2); tt = t8
    var hi: [i32; 8] = [0; 8]
    i = 0; while i < 8 { hi[i as usize] = hi_raw[i as usize]; i = i + 1 }

    var out: [i32; 16] = [0; 16]
    i = 0
    while i < 8 { out[i as usize] = lo[i as usize]; out[(8 + i) as usize] = hi[i as usize]; i = i + 1 }
    (out, tt)
}

fn real16_mul(t: Tape, a: [i32; 16], b: [i32; 16]) -> ([i32; 16], Tape) with Mut, Div, Panic {
    var tt = t
    var out: [i32; 16] = [0; 16]
    var i: i64 = 0
    while i < 16 {
        let (idx, tn) = tmul(tt, a[i as usize], b[i as usize])
        tt = tn
        out[i as usize] = idx
        i = i + 1
    }
    (out, tt)
}

fn vec16_add(t: Tape, a: [i32; 16], b: [i32; 16]) -> ([i32; 16], Tape) with Mut, Div, Panic {
    var tt = t
    var out: [i32; 16] = [0; 16]
    var i: i64 = 0
    while i < 16 { let (idx, tn) = tadd(tt, a[i as usize], b[i as usize]); tt = tn; out[i as usize] = idx; i = i + 1 }
    (out, tt)
}
fn vec16_tanh(t: Tape, a: [i32; 16]) -> ([i32; 16], Tape) with Mut, Div, Panic {
    var tt = t
    var out: [i32; 16] = [0; 16]
    var i: i64 = 0
    while i < 16 { let (idx, tn) = ttanh(tt, a[i as usize]); tt = tn; out[i as usize] = idx; i = i + 1 }
    (out, tt)
}

// =============================================================================
// CORRECTNESS CHECK: sed16_mul evaluated on the tape (do_update never
// called -- just forward pass, node values read directly) must satisfy
// the two identities this investigation's count_anticommutativity_
// violations() uses to certify a sedenion table: e_i*e_i=-e_0 (i>=1),
// e_i*e_j=-(e_j*e_i) (i!=j, both >=1). Any violation here means sed16_mul
// is wired wrong and nothing trained with it should be trusted.
// =============================================================================

fn basis16_nodes(t: Tape, k: i64) -> ([i32; 16], Tape) with Mut, Div, Panic {
    var tt = t
    var out: [i32; 16] = [0; 16]
    var i: i64 = 0
    while i < 16 {
        let v = if i == k { 1.0 } else { 0.0 }
        let (idx, tn) = tvar(tt, v)
        tt = tn
        out[i as usize] = idx
        i = i + 1
    }
    (out, tt)
}

fn verify_sed16() -> (i64, i64) with IO, Mut, Div, Panic {
    var pass: i64 = 0
    var fail: i64 = 0
    // e_i * e_i = -e_0, i = 1..15
    var i: i64 = 1
    while i < 16 {
        var t = tape_new()
        let (ei, t1) = basis16_nodes(t, i); t = t1
        let (prod, t2) = sed16_mul(t, ei, ei); t = t2
        let ok0 = t.values[prod[0] as usize] < 0.0 - 0.999999 && t.values[prod[0] as usize] > 0.0 - 1.000001
        var ok_rest = true
        var k: i64 = 1
        while k < 16 {
            let v = t.values[prod[k as usize] as usize]
            if v > 0.000001 || v < 0.0 - 0.000001 { ok_rest = false }
            k = k + 1
        }
        if ok0 && ok_rest { pass = pass + 1 } else { fail = fail + 1 }
        i = i + 1
    }
    // e_i*e_j = -(e_j*e_i), 1<=i<j<=15
    i = 1
    while i < 16 {
        var j: i64 = i + 1
        while j < 16 {
            var t = tape_new()
            let (ei_raw, t1) = basis16_nodes(t, i); t = t1
            var ei: [i32; 16] = [0; 16]
            var pc: i64 = 0
            while pc < 16 { ei[pc as usize] = ei_raw[pc as usize]; pc = pc + 1 }

            let (ej_raw, t2) = basis16_nodes(t, j); t = t2
            var ej: [i32; 16] = [0; 16]
            pc = 0; while pc < 16 { ej[pc as usize] = ej_raw[pc as usize]; pc = pc + 1 }

            let (pij_raw, t3) = sed16_mul(t, ei, ej); t = t3
            var pij: [i32; 16] = [0; 16]
            pc = 0; while pc < 16 { pij[pc as usize] = pij_raw[pc as usize]; pc = pc + 1 }

            let (eii_raw, t4) = basis16_nodes(t, i); t = t4
            var eii: [i32; 16] = [0; 16]
            pc = 0; while pc < 16 { eii[pc as usize] = eii_raw[pc as usize]; pc = pc + 1 }

            let (ejj_raw, t5) = basis16_nodes(t, j); t = t5
            var ejj: [i32; 16] = [0; 16]
            pc = 0; while pc < 16 { ejj[pc as usize] = ejj_raw[pc as usize]; pc = pc + 1 }

            let (pji_raw, t6) = sed16_mul(t, ejj, eii); t = t6
            var pji: [i32; 16] = [0; 16]
            pc = 0; while pc < 16 { pji[pc as usize] = pji_raw[pc as usize]; pc = pc + 1 }

            var ok = true
            var k: i64 = 0
            while k < 16 {
                let vij = t.values[pij[k as usize] as usize]
                let vji = t.values[pji[k as usize] as usize]
                let d = vij + vji
                let ad = if d < 0.0 { 0.0 - d } else { d }
                if ad > 0.000001 { ok = false }
                k = k + 1
            }
            if ok { pass = pass + 1 } else { fail = fail + 1 }
            j = j + 1
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    (pass, fail)
}

// =============================================================================
// MINIMAL NESTED-VS-CROSSED TASK, length 4 (SedenTree analogue of the
// OctTree file's Dyck-4: the smallest depth-2 case). Tokens: 0=unused,
// 1='(', 2=')', 3='[', 4=']'. nested="([])"-order, crossed="([)]"-order
// (or the bracket-type-swapped equivalent), matching the topological
// distinction examples/cayley_dickson_dyck_pseudoknot_generators.sio's
// gen_pseudoknot uses at full length 32 -- see this file's header for why
// length 4 instead of 32.
// =============================================================================

fn gen_pk4(nested: bool, out: &![i64; 4]) with Mut, Div, Panic {
    let paren_first = rng_bit()
    if paren_first {
        out[0] = 1; out[1] = 3
        if nested { out[2] = 4; out[3] = 2 } else { out[2] = 2; out[3] = 4 }
    } else {
        out[0] = 3; out[1] = 1
        if nested { out[2] = 2; out[3] = 4 } else { out[2] = 4; out[3] = 2 }
    }
}

// Trivially separable diagnostic task (label = token[0]), used only to
// confirm the training loop/architecture below CAN learn something at
// all before trusting a null result on the real (gen_pk4) task as
// evidence the task is hard rather than evidence the loop is broken.
// Other 3 tokens are random noise from the same {1,2,3,4} vocabulary so
// the model cannot shortcut via sequence length or token-set alone.
fn gen_trivial(label1: bool, out: &![i64; 4]) with Mut, Div, Panic {
    out[0] = if label1 { 1 } else { 2 }
    var k: i64 = 1
    while k < 4 { out[k as usize] = 1 + rng_nonneg(4); k = k + 1 }
}

// =============================================================================
// PARAMETERS. EMBED: 5 tokens x 16 dims. WOUT: 16 dims x 2 classes.
// =============================================================================

var EMBED: [f64; 80] = [0.0; 80]
var WOUT: [f64; 32] = [0.0; 32]
var BOUT: [f64; 2] = [0.0; 2]

fn init_params() with Mut, Div {
    var i: i64 = 0
    while i < 80 { EMBED[i as usize] = 0.15 * rng_next_signed(); i = i + 1 }
    i = 0
    while i < 32 { WOUT[i as usize] = 0.15 * rng_next_signed(); i = i + 1 }
    i = 0
    while i < 2 { BOUT[i as usize] = 0.0; i = i + 1 }
}

// =============================================================================
// ONE TRAINING/EVAL STEP (mirrors the OctTree file's step/step_p exactly,
// at 16 dims and with sed16_mul/real16_mul instead of oct8_mul/real8_mul)
// =============================================================================

fn step(tokens: &[i64; 4], label: i64, use_sed: bool, lr: f64, do_update: bool, out: &![f64; 2]) with Mut, Div, Panic {
    var probe: [f64; 1] = [0.0; 1]
    step_p(tokens, label, use_sed, lr, do_update, out, 0 - 1, &!probe)
}

fn step_p(tokens: &[i64; 4], label: i64, use_sed: bool, lr: f64, do_update: bool, out: &![f64; 2], probe_pidx: i64, out_probe: &![f64; 1]) with Mut, Div, Panic {
    var t = tape_new()
    var leaf_node: [i32; 64] = [0; 64]   // 4 positions x 16 dims
    var leaf_param: [i64; 64] = [0; 64]
    var li: i64 = 0
    // Flat, not [[i32;16];4] -- a nested-array local was rejected by the
    // compiler here ("array index must be integer") even though an
    // identical-shape [[i32;8];4] compiles fine in the OctTree file;
    // avoided rather than chased, matching this investigation's standing
    // nested-array workaround pattern (see the LEMON FFI file's B: [f64;56]
    // note). leaf0..leaf3 are built directly instead of through a container.
    var leaf0: [i32; 16] = [0; 16]
    var leaf1: [i32; 16] = [0; 16]
    var leaf2: [i32; 16] = [0; 16]
    var leaf3: [i32; 16] = [0; 16]
    while li < 4 {
        let tok = tokens[li as usize]
        var d: i64 = 0
        while d < 16 {
            let pidx = tok * 16 + d
            let (idx, tn) = tvar(t, EMBED[pidx as usize])
            t = tn
            if li == 0 { leaf0[d as usize] = idx }
            else if li == 1 { leaf1[d as usize] = idx }
            else if li == 2 { leaf2[d as usize] = idx }
            else { leaf3[d as usize] = idx }
            leaf_node[(li * 16 + d) as usize] = idx
            leaf_param[(li * 16 + d) as usize] = pidx
            d = d + 1
        }
        li = li + 1
    }

    var h1: [i32; 16] = [0; 16]
    var h2: [i32; 16] = [0; 16]
    if use_sed {
        let (p1, t1) = sed16_mul(t, leaf0, leaf1); t = t1
        let (r1, t1b) = vec16_add(t, leaf0, leaf1); t = t1b
        let (s1, t1c) = vec16_add(t, p1, r1); t = t1c
        let (o1, t1d) = vec16_tanh(t, s1); t = t1d
        h1 = o1
        let (p2, t2) = sed16_mul(t, leaf2, leaf3); t = t2
        let (r2, t2b) = vec16_add(t, leaf2, leaf3); t = t2b
        let (s2, t2c) = vec16_add(t, p2, r2); t = t2c
        let (o2, t2d) = vec16_tanh(t, s2); t = t2d
        h2 = o2
    } else {
        let (p1, t1) = real16_mul(t, leaf0, leaf1); t = t1
        let (r1, t1b) = vec16_add(t, leaf0, leaf1); t = t1b
        let (s1, t1c) = vec16_add(t, p1, r1); t = t1c
        let (o1, t1d) = vec16_tanh(t, s1); t = t1d
        h1 = o1
        let (p2, t2) = real16_mul(t, leaf2, leaf3); t = t2
        let (r2, t2b) = vec16_add(t, leaf2, leaf3); t = t2b
        let (s2, t2c) = vec16_add(t, p2, r2); t = t2c
        let (o2, t2d) = vec16_tanh(t, s2); t = t2d
        h2 = o2
    }

    var root: [i32; 16] = [0; 16]
    if use_sed {
        let (p3, t3) = sed16_mul(t, h1, h2); t = t3
        let (r3, t3b) = vec16_add(t, h1, h2); t = t3b
        let (s3, t3c) = vec16_add(t, p3, r3); t = t3c
        let (o3, t3d) = vec16_tanh(t, s3); t = t3d
        root = o3
    } else {
        let (p3, t3) = real16_mul(t, h1, h2); t = t3
        let (r3, t3b) = vec16_add(t, h1, h2); t = t3b
        let (s3, t3c) = vec16_add(t, p3, r3); t = t3c
        let (o3, t3d) = vec16_tanh(t, s3); t = t3d
        root = o3
    }

    var wnode: [i32; 32] = [0; 32]
    var bnode: [i32; 2] = [0; 2]
    var c: i64 = 0
    while c < 2 { let (bi, tn) = tvar(t, BOUT[c as usize]); t = tn; bnode[c as usize] = bi; c = c + 1 }
    var logit: [i32; 2] = [0; 2]
    c = 0
    while c < 2 {
        var acc = bnode[c as usize]
        var k: i64 = 0
        while k < 16 {
            let widx = k * 2 + c
            let (wi, tn) = tvar(t, WOUT[widx as usize]); t = tn
            wnode[widx as usize] = wi
            let (mi, tn2) = tmul(t, root[k as usize], wi); t = tn2
            let (ai, tn3) = tadd(t, acc, mi); t = tn3
            acc = ai
            k = k + 1
        }
        logit[c as usize] = acc
        c = c + 1
    }

    let (e0, ta) = texp(t, logit[0]); t = ta
    let (e1, tb) = texp(t, logit[1]); t = tb
    let (s, tc) = tadd(t, e0, e1); t = tc
    let etrue = if label == 0 { e0 } else { e1 }
    let (p, td) = tdiv(t, etrue, s); t = td
    let (lp, te) = tlog(t, p); t = te
    let (loss_node, tf) = tneg(t, lp); t = tf

    let loss_val = t.values[loss_node as usize]
    let pred = if t.values[logit[1] as usize] > t.values[logit[0] as usize] { 1.0 } else { 0.0 }
    out[0] = loss_val
    out[1] = pred

    if do_update || probe_pidx >= 0 {
        t = tape_backward(t, loss_node)
        if probe_pidx >= 0 {
            var pg = 0.0
            var pi: i64 = 0
            while pi < 64 {
                if leaf_param[pi as usize] == probe_pidx { pg = pg + t.grads[leaf_node[pi as usize] as usize] }
                pi = pi + 1
            }
            out_probe[0] = pg
        }
        if do_update {
            var i: i64 = 0
            while i < 64 {
                let g = t.grads[leaf_node[i as usize] as usize]
                let pidx = leaf_param[i as usize]
                EMBED[pidx as usize] = EMBED[pidx as usize] - lr * g
                i = i + 1
            }
            i = 0
            while i < 32 {
                let g = t.grads[wnode[i as usize] as usize]
                WOUT[i as usize] = WOUT[i as usize] - lr * g
                i = i + 1
            }
            i = 0
            while i < 2 {
                let g = t.grads[bnode[i as usize] as usize]
                BOUT[i as usize] = BOUT[i as usize] - lr * g
                i = i + 1
            }
        }
    }
}

fn grad_check(tokens: &[i64; 4], label: i64, use_sed: bool, probe_pidx: i64) with IO, Mut, Div, Panic {
    let eps = 0.0001
    let saved = EMBED[probe_pidx as usize]

    var out_plus: [f64; 2] = [0.0; 2]
    var dummy: [f64; 1] = [0.0; 1]
    EMBED[probe_pidx as usize] = saved + eps
    step_p(tokens, label, use_sed, 0.0, false, &!out_plus, 0 - 1, &!dummy)

    var out_minus: [f64; 2] = [0.0; 2]
    EMBED[probe_pidx as usize] = saved - eps
    step_p(tokens, label, use_sed, 0.0, false, &!out_minus, 0 - 1, &!dummy)

    EMBED[probe_pidx as usize] = saved
    var out_analytic: [f64; 2] = [0.0; 2]
    var probe: [f64; 1] = [0.0; 1]
    step_p(tokens, label, use_sed, 0.0, false, &!out_analytic, probe_pidx, &!probe)

    let numeric_grad = (out_plus[0] - out_minus[0]) / (2.0 * eps)
    let analytic_grad = probe[0]
    let diff = if numeric_grad > analytic_grad { numeric_grad - analytic_grad } else { analytic_grad - numeric_grad }
    print("  probe EMBED["); print_i64(probe_pidx); print("]: numeric="); print_f64(numeric_grad, 6)
    print("  analytic="); print_f64(analytic_grad, 6); print("  |diff|="); print_f64(diff, 8)
    if diff < 0.001 { print("  MATCH\n") } else { print("  MISMATCH -- DO NOT TRUST TRAINING RESULTS\n") }
}

// =============================================================================
// FULL TRAINING RUN. Scoped DOWN from the OctTree file's N_TRAIN=60/
// EPOCHS=25/N_SEEDS=4 as a first attempt, since sed16_mul's tape footprint
// is ~3.6x oct8_mul's and that file already found a hard resource ceiling
// near ~15,000 cumulative train steps at the smaller footprint. Smoke-test
// scale first; scale up only if this completes and reports honestly if it
// does not.
// =============================================================================

const N_PER_CLASS: i64 = 20
const N_TEST_PER_CLASS: i64 = 10
const N_TRAIN: i64 = 40
const N_TEST: i64 = 20
const EPOCHS: i64 = 15
const LR: f64 = 0.05

// Flat, not [[i64;4];40]/[[i64;4];20] -- a nested-array global here
// corrupted an ADJACENT global (TRAIN_LBL read back garbage/stack-address
// values after build_dataset(), e.g. 140735830200840), matching the
// already-documented [[T;N];M] nested-array defect class from
// examples/cayley_dickson_g2_derivation_basis.sio (element-write segfault)
// and examples/cayley_dickson_lemon_g2_ffi.sio (function-parameter hang) --
// a third distinct manifestation (cross-global corruption) of the same
// underlying fragility. Flattened with manual i*4+k offset indexing.
var TRAIN_TOK: [i64; 160] = [0; 160]
var TRAIN_LBL: [i64; 40] = [0; 40]
var TEST_TOK: [i64; 80] = [0; 80]
var TEST_LBL: [i64; 20] = [0; 20]

fn build_dataset() with Mut, Div, Panic {
    var i: i64 = 0
    while i < N_PER_CLASS {
        var tk: [i64; 4] = [0; 4]
        gen_pk4(true, &!tk)
        var k: i64 = 0
        while k < 4 { TRAIN_TOK[(i * 4 + k) as usize] = tk[k as usize]; k = k + 1 }
        TRAIN_LBL[i as usize] = 1
        i = i + 1
    }
    while i < 2 * N_PER_CLASS {
        var tk: [i64; 4] = [0; 4]
        gen_pk4(false, &!tk)
        var k: i64 = 0
        while k < 4 { TRAIN_TOK[(i * 4 + k) as usize] = tk[k as usize]; k = k + 1 }
        TRAIN_LBL[i as usize] = 0
        i = i + 1
    }
    i = 0
    while i < N_TEST_PER_CLASS {
        var tk: [i64; 4] = [0; 4]
        gen_pk4(true, &!tk)
        var k: i64 = 0
        while k < 4 { TEST_TOK[(i * 4 + k) as usize] = tk[k as usize]; k = k + 1 }
        TEST_LBL[i as usize] = 1
        i = i + 1
    }
    while i < 2 * N_TEST_PER_CLASS {
        var tk: [i64; 4] = [0; 4]
        gen_pk4(false, &!tk)
        var k: i64 = 0
        while k < 4 { TEST_TOK[(i * 4 + k) as usize] = tk[k as usize]; k = k + 1 }
        TEST_LBL[i as usize] = 0
        i = i + 1
    }
}

// Same shape as build_dataset() but from gen_trivial -- used only for the
// pre-flight sanity check in main() (does the loop learn ANYTHING?).
fn build_dataset_trivial() with Mut, Div, Panic {
    var i: i64 = 0
    while i < N_PER_CLASS {
        var tk: [i64; 4] = [0; 4]
        gen_trivial(true, &!tk)
        var k: i64 = 0
        while k < 4 { TRAIN_TOK[(i * 4 + k) as usize] = tk[k as usize]; k = k + 1 }
        TRAIN_LBL[i as usize] = 1
        i = i + 1
    }
    while i < 2 * N_PER_CLASS {
        var tk: [i64; 4] = [0; 4]
        gen_trivial(false, &!tk)
        var k: i64 = 0
        while k < 4 { TRAIN_TOK[(i * 4 + k) as usize] = tk[k as usize]; k = k + 1 }
        TRAIN_LBL[i as usize] = 0
        i = i + 1
    }
    i = 0
    while i < N_TEST_PER_CLASS {
        var tk: [i64; 4] = [0; 4]
        gen_trivial(true, &!tk)
        var k: i64 = 0
        while k < 4 { TEST_TOK[(i * 4 + k) as usize] = tk[k as usize]; k = k + 1 }
        TEST_LBL[i as usize] = 1
        i = i + 1
    }
    while i < 2 * N_TEST_PER_CLASS {
        var tk: [i64; 4] = [0; 4]
        gen_trivial(false, &!tk)
        var k: i64 = 0
        while k < 4 { TEST_TOK[(i * 4 + k) as usize] = tk[k as usize]; k = k + 1 }
        TEST_LBL[i as usize] = 0
        i = i + 1
    }
}

fn eval_accuracy(use_sed: bool, on_train: bool) -> f64 with Mut, Div, Panic {
    let n = if on_train { N_TRAIN } else { N_TEST }
    var correct: i64 = 0
    var i: i64 = 0
    while i < n {
        var tk: [i64; 4] = [0; 4]
        var k: i64 = 0
        while k < 4 {
            tk[k as usize] = if on_train { TRAIN_TOK[(i * 4 + k) as usize] } else { TEST_TOK[(i * 4 + k) as usize] }
            k = k + 1
        }
        let lbl = if on_train { TRAIN_LBL[i as usize] } else { TEST_LBL[i as usize] }
        var out: [f64; 2] = [0.0; 2]
        step(&tk, lbl, use_sed, 0.0, false, &!out)
        if (out[1] as i64) == lbl { correct = correct + 1 }
        i = i + 1
    }
    (correct as f64) / (n as f64)
}

fn train_model(use_sed: bool, seed: i64) with IO, Mut, Div, Panic {
    rng_seed(seed)
    init_params()
    var ep: i64 = 0
    while ep < EPOCHS {
        var epoch_loss = 0.0
        var i: i64 = 0
        while i < N_TRAIN {
            var tk: [i64; 4] = [0; 4]
            var k: i64 = 0
            while k < 4 { tk[k as usize] = TRAIN_TOK[(i * 4 + k) as usize]; k = k + 1 }
            let lbl = TRAIN_LBL[i as usize]
            var out: [f64; 2] = [0.0; 2]
            step(&tk, lbl, use_sed, LR, true, &!out)
            epoch_loss = epoch_loss + out[0]
            i = i + 1
        }
        if (ep + 1) - ((ep + 1) / 5) * 5 == 0 || ep == 0 {
            let tr_acc = eval_accuracy(use_sed, true)
            let te_acc = eval_accuracy(use_sed, false)
            print("    epoch "); print_i64(ep + 1); print("/"); print_i64(EPOCHS)
            print("  mean_train_loss="); print_f64(epoch_loss / (N_TRAIN as f64), 4)
            print("  train_acc="); print_f64(tr_acc, 3); print("  test_acc="); print_f64(te_acc, 3); print("\n")
        }
        ep = ep + 1
    }
}

fn train_model_quiet(use_sed: bool, seed: i64) with Mut, Div, Panic {
    rng_seed(seed)
    init_params()
    var ep: i64 = 0
    while ep < EPOCHS {
        var i: i64 = 0
        while i < N_TRAIN {
            var tk: [i64; 4] = [0; 4]
            var k: i64 = 0
            while k < 4 { tk[k as usize] = TRAIN_TOK[(i * 4 + k) as usize]; k = k + 1 }
            let lbl = TRAIN_LBL[i as usize]
            var out: [f64; 2] = [0.0; 2]
            step(&tk, lbl, use_sed, LR, true, &!out)
            i = i + 1
        }
        ep = ep + 1
    }
}

const N_SEEDS: i64 = 2

fn main() -> i32 with IO, Mut, Div, Panic {
    // Local, not a module-level global -- see the N_GEN-style comment
    // pattern already documented elsewhere in this investigation
    // (examples/cayley_dickson_lemon_g2_ffi.sio's bug #6): a global array
    // read with a runtime loop index reliably returns element 0. Found
    // HERE too: the multi-seed loop below printed "seed=20260815" twice
    // (should have been 20260815 then 7) until SEED_LIST was made local.
    var seed_list: [i64; 2] = [20260815, 7]
    print("=== SedenTree vs RealTree-16, TRAINED, minimal length-4 nested-vs-crossed task ===\n")
    print("(scope disclosed in file header: length 4/depth 2 only, no gates, plain SGD, small n)\n")

    print("\n[sed16_mul correctness check: e_i*e_i=-e_0 and e_i*e_j=-(e_j*e_i), i,j=1..15]\n")
    let (vpass, vfail) = verify_sed16()
    print("  pass="); print_i64(vpass); print("  fail="); print_i64(vfail)
    print("  (expect pass=120, fail=0: 15 self-products + 105 antisymmetric pairs)\n")
    if vfail > 0 {
        print("\n  sed16_mul FAILED correctness check -- ABORTING, do not trust any further output.\n")
        return 1
    }

    rng_seed(20260815)
    init_params()
    print("\n[Gradient check] finite-difference vs analytic:\n")
    var gc_tokens: [i64; 4] = [1, 3, 4, 2]
    grad_check(&gc_tokens, 1, true, 16)
    grad_check(&gc_tokens, 1, false, 16)
    var gc_tokens2: [i64; 4] = [1, 3, 2, 4]
    grad_check(&gc_tokens2, 0, true, 32)
    grad_check(&gc_tokens2, 0, false, 32)

    print("\n[Sanity check: CAN the training loop learn ANYTHING?] trivial\n")
    print("token[0]-determines-label task, same architecture/optimizer,\n")
    print("before trusting any null result on the real (harder) task below:\n")
    rng_seed(999)
    build_dataset_trivial()
    train_model(true, 20260815)
    let sanity_acc = eval_accuracy(true, false)
    print("  [SedenTree, trivial task] final test_acc="); print_f64(sanity_acc, 3)
    if sanity_acc > 0.9 { print("  -- loop CAN learn; the real task's null result below is about task difficulty, not a broken loop\n") }
    else { print("  -- loop did NOT solve even the trivial task; treat the null result below as suspect, not just \"hard\"\n") }

    rng_seed(999)
    build_dataset()
    var v0: i64 = 0
    var i: i64 = 0
    while i < N_TRAIN { if TRAIN_LBL[i as usize] == 1 { v0 = v0 + 1 }; i = i + 1 }
    print("\n[Dataset] N_train="); print_i64(N_TRAIN); print(" (nested="); print_i64(v0)
    print(", crossed="); print_i64(N_TRAIN - v0); print(")  N_test="); print_i64(N_TEST); print("\n")

    print("\n[Single-seed detail, seed="); print_i64(seed_list[0]); print("]\n")
    print("\n  [SedenTree]\n")
    train_model(true, seed_list[0])
    print("\n  [RealTree-16]\n")
    train_model(false, seed_list[0])

    print("\n[Multi-seed summary, "); print_i64(N_SEEDS); print(" seeds, ")
    print_i64(EPOCHS); print(" epochs each]\n")
    var sed_sum = 0.0
    var real_sum = 0.0
    var s: i64 = 0
    while s < N_SEEDS {
        let seed = seed_list[s as usize]
        train_model_quiet(true, seed)
        let sed_te = eval_accuracy(true, false)
        train_model_quiet(false, seed)
        let real_te = eval_accuracy(false, false)
        print("  seed="); print_i64(seed); print("  SedenTree test_acc=")
        print_f64(sed_te, 3); print("  RealTree-16 test_acc="); print_f64(real_te, 3); print("\n")
        sed_sum = sed_sum + sed_te
        real_sum = real_sum + real_te
        s = s + 1
    }
    print("\n[Final] mean over "); print_i64(N_SEEDS); print(" seeds:\n")
    print("  SedenTree   mean test_acc="); print_f64(sed_sum / (N_SEEDS as f64), 3); print("\n")
    print("  RealTree-16 mean test_acc="); print_f64(real_sum / (N_SEEDS as f64), 3); print("\n")

    print("\n=== DONE ===\n")
    0
}

// =============================================================================
// FOUR COMPILER BUGS FOUND WHILE BUILDING THIS FILE (none patched in
// self-hosted/, all worked around here -- flagged for this repo's
// forensic-dispatch process). Full repro/isolation detail lives inline
// near each fix; this is an index, not a duplicate.
// =============================================================================
//
// 1. Same-shaped local array `let`-bindings alias each other's storage.
//    See sed16_mul()'s leading comment above its definition: reading a
//    `let`-bound `[i32;8]` a second time, after a second same-shaped
//    `[i32;8]` `let` binding was computed in between, silently returned
//    the SECOND binding's values -- isolated via direct tape-index prints
//    (`p[0]` read as index 86 immediately after computing it, then index
//    213 -- literally `q`'s own index -- after `q` was computed, with no
//    reassignment of `p` in between). A `var` declared directly and
//    filled via an explicit index loop does not exhibit this. Fixed by
//    converting every multi-use `[i32;8]`/`[i32;16]` intermediate in
//    sed16_mul() and verify_sed16()'s antisymmetry loop to that pattern.
//
// 2. A too-small TAPE_CAP silently wraps tape writes instead of erroring.
//    Debugging bug #1 involved temporarily lowering TAPE_CAP to 1024;
//    verify_sed16()'s antisymmetry check (which needs ~1084 nodes) kept
//    failing 44/105 pairs with a value pattern that looked like a NEW,
//    second aliasing bug -- it was actually simple overflow: `tpush()`
//    had no bounds check, so writes past index 1023 landed wherever
//    `idx as usize` happened to wrap/collide, producing plausible-looking
//    but wrong output rather than a crash or error. `tpush()` now
//    hard-aborts (`panic()`) if `len >= TAPE_CAP` rather than continuing.
//    TAPE_CAP is 4096, with >2x margin over both the ~1794-node step_p()
//    forward+backward pass and the ~1084-node antisymmetry check.
//
// 3. A `[[T;N];M]` nested-array GLOBAL corrupted an ADJACENT global.
//    `var TRAIN_TOK: [[i64;4];40]` followed by `var TRAIN_LBL: [i64;40]`
//    -- after build_dataset() ran, TRAIN_LBL read back garbage/stack-
//    address-looking values (e.g. 140735830200840) instead of the 0s/1s
//    it was written with, even though build_dataset()'s own write loop
//    for TRAIN_LBL looks correct in isolation. This is a third, distinct
//    manifestation of the nested-array defect class already found twice
//    elsewhere in this investigation (examples/cayley_dickson_g2_
//    derivation_basis.sio: element-write segfault; examples/cayley_
//    dickson_lemon_g2_ffi.sio: function-parameter hang) -- this time the
//    corruption crosses into a NEIGHBORING global rather than staying
//    within the nested array itself. Fixed by flattening TRAIN_TOK/
//    TEST_TOK to `[i64;160]`/`[i64;80]` with manual `i*4+k` offset
//    indexing, matching this investigation's standing workaround for the
//    whole nested-array class.
//
// 4. A module-level `var arr: [T;N]` array read with a RUNTIME loop index
//    always returns element 0. `var SEED_LIST: [i64;2] = [20260815, 7]`,
//    read via `SEED_LIST[s as usize]` inside the multi-seed loop, printed
//    "seed=20260815" for BOTH iterations instead of 20260815 then 7 --
//    the exact same defect already found and documented in examples/
//    cayley_dickson_lemon_g2_ffi.sio's GEN_A/GEN_B bug (write-once-at-
//    init, read-many-with-computed-index global arrays are affected;
//    write-every-iteration globals like TRAIN_LBL above are not). Fixed
//    the same way: made local to main() (`var seed_list`), no parameter
//    threading needed since it is only ever read within that function.
